# DTF tier manifest: backup-exclude-repos  (#2772)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. A backup archive is a plain object written
# by StorageService (filesystem backend rooted at /data/storage); the set of
# artifacts it contains is chosen by BackupService::get_artifact_storage_keys
# straight from the `artifacts` table, so the exclusion behaviour is
# backend-agnostic and runs on the default filesystem overlay. The archive's
# artifact_count is recorded on the completed `backups` row and asserted from
# the DB, and the archive's own objects live on disk in the backend container.
#
# The feature (#2772): airgapped / low-bandwidth operators want to leave large
# or non-critical repositories out of a Full/Incremental backup.
# CreateBackupRequest gains an optional `exclude_repository_ids` list; artifacts
# belonging to those repositories are skipped during the backup and the
# exclusion is recorded in the `backups` row metadata. Empty/absent list ==
# exclude nothing (no behaviour change). A repository may not appear in both the
# include (`repository_ids`) and exclude lists (contradiction -> 400).
# Pre-#2772 the `exclude_repository_ids` field does not exist, so it is silently
# ignored: every repository is backed up regardless, the metadata carries no
# exclusion, and the contradictory include/exclude request is accepted.
#
# Oracle (oracle.sh) is the discriminating gate. It seeds two maven local repos
# with one real .jar each, then:
#   * BEHAVIOURAL — a full backup (no exclusion) counts N artifacts; a backup
#     excluding one repo counts strictly fewer (exactly the excluded repo's
#     stored artifacts are dropped).
#     Pre-#2772: the exclude list is ignored -> the excluding backup counts the
#     same N -> FAIL.
#   * METADATA — the excluding backup's `backups.metadata` records the excluded
#     repository id. Pre-#2772: no exclusion key is present -> FAIL.
#   * REJECTION — a create-backup naming the same repo in BOTH include and
#     exclude is rejected 400. Pre-#2772: the unknown field is ignored and the
#     request is accepted (2xx) -> FAIL.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives admin login + repo creation +
# maven PUTs + several admin create/execute-backup calls back-to-back; the base
# default (ON) could throttle them.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
